ces of forming these Chows? For example, if I discard W9, which is a terminal tile, maybe that's a safe discard. W9 is a high tile, and there's 4 remaining. But keeping it might not help in forming any melds. Alternatively, if I discard J3, but that's part of the pair. So probably not a good idea. The J3 pair is important for the hand.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles: J3 has 4 remaining. I have two. So there are two more J3s out there. If I discard J3, someone else might take it, but if I need a pair, it's better to keep both.\n\nSo the possible tiles to discard are B5, W2, W9, B6, T5, B7, T2, J3, W3, T4.\n\nIf I discard W9, which is a terminal, maybe that's a safe play. It doesn't interfere with any potential melds. Alternatively, if I discard T2, I lose the chance to form a T2-T3-T4 Chow. But T3 has 3 remaining. If I keep T2 and T4, I can wait for T3. Similarly, if I discard T4, I lose the T2-T3-T4 possibility. So maybe keeping both is better.\n\nLooking at the B5, B6, B7. If I discard any of them, I break the potential Chow. So maybe keeping all three is better. So perhaps the best tile to discard is W9, since it's not part of any sequence. Alternatively, maybe W2 or W3, but they are part of the potential W2-W3-W4 Chow. Discarding W2 would leave W3, but then I can't form that Chow. Similarly for W3.\n\nSo if I discard W9, I can keep all the potential sequences intact. Let me check the unshown tiles for W9: W9 has 4 remaining. So it's a safe discard. Also, W9 is a terminal tile, which might be less useful for forming melds. So discarding W9 could be a good move.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T5, but I have T5 in the meld and in the hand. Wait, the meld is [T5, T6, T7], so the T5 is already part of the meld. The T5 in the hand is an extra one. If I discard that T5, I might be able to form another meld. Wait, but I already have a T5 in the meld. If I discard the extra T5, maybe someone else can take it for a Pung. But if I keep it, it's just a single tile. Not sure