ng chow is T5-6-7. So T3 is in hand.\n\n- B7 is part of the B6-B7-B8 chow. So that's already formed.\n\n- J1 is a single. Maybe if I can get another J1 to form a pair or pung. But there's only one left in the unshown tiles (J1:1). If I play J1, I can't form a pung, but maybe a pair if I draw another.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a pung with some tiles. For example, if I have two of the same, but I don't have any duplicates except possibly if I play a tile and someone else discards it. But since I need to play a tile, I need to see which play would leave me with better chances.\n\nLooking at the possible plays:\n\nIf I play W6, then my hand becomes [W1, W3, W5, T3, T7, B7, J1]. Now, I have seven tiles. Let's see if I can form the necessary melds.\n\nI need two more melds and a pair. Let's see:\n\n- The existing two chows are [T5-T6-T7] and [B6-B7-B8]. So that's two.\n\nNow, from the remaining tiles: W1, W3, W5, T3, T7, B7, J1.\n\nPossible options:\n\n- T3 could be part of a chow if I have T1-T2-T3, but I don't have those. Or T3-T4-T5, but I don't have T4 or T5. Wait, I have T5 in the meld. So maybe not.\n\n- W1, W3, W5: No chow.\n\n- J1 is a single. If I can form a pair with J1, but only one left. If I draw another, then pair.\n\nAlternatively, maybe form a pung of B7? But I have one B7 in hand. The meld has B7 in it. Wait, the meld is [B6, B7, B8], which is a chow. So the B7 in hand is separate. So if I have B7 in hand, and the meld has B7, but that's part of the chow. So I can't form a pung with B7 unless I have two more. But there's one left in unshown (B7:1). So if I keep B7, maybe I can form a pung if I draw another. But since I'm playing a tile, I'm discarding one. If I play B7, I lose the chance to form a pung.\n\nAlternatively, maybe playing W6 allows me to keep B7 for a possible pung. But I need to see.\n\nAnother angle: The current hand has J1. If I play J1, I lose that tile. But if I keep J1, maybe I can form a pair with another J1. Since there's one